            <ArticleTitle>Statin therapy does not improve lung function in asthma: a meta-analysis of randomized controlled trials.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>276-83</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1177/0300060513477005</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To assess the efficacy of statins in the treatment of asthma.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Electronic databases were searched to identify randomized controlled trials that measured the efficacy of statins in the treatment of asthma, and a meta-analysis of the pooled data was performed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Five trials were identified; four met the inclusion criteria (total number of patients 200). Compared with controls, patients in the statin groups had higher forced expiratory volume in 1 s (FEV1) values before inhaled corticosteroids (0.09 l, 95% confidence interval [CI] -0.06, 0.23), higher FEV1 values after inhaled corticosteroids (0.06 l, 95% CI -0.09, 0.22), and higher morning peak expiratory flow rates (9.87 l, 95% CI -15.66, 35.40). These results were not statistically significant and, furthermore, publication bias was detected.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">In conclusion, there is currently insufficient evidence to show that statins improve lung function in patients with asthma.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
